import "html_components.fx".

DocsStdlib() =>
    guidance := RenderGuidance(doText: "Import only the modules an example uses, prefer helper methods for common shapes, and keep named arguments visible in every call.", dontText: "Do not grow one-off interpreter behavior when a core module or native-backed helper can own the capability cleanly.", recommendText: "Use core/*.fx files as the public contract: declarations expose primitives, helper methods make applications readable, and native code does heavy I/O or numeric work."),
    return (HtmlRichSectionData(id: "stdlib", title: "Standard Library", p: "Core modules are imported as Felidae libraries. Native-backed modules include file, http, json, csv, str, array, math, db, probability, ml, process, and thread. system is available automatically. Some modules also expose Felidae helper methods on top of native calls; for example core/http.fx declares raw http.* operations and higher-level HttpResponse/HttpServe helpers.", p2: "The stdlib keeps heavy work behind module boundaries. Felidae source declares callable shapes, while native implementations handle file I/O, HTTP, CSV, math, machine-learning helpers, probability routines, and process helpers. Prefer helper methods when they make application code more data-shaped, and use raw native calls when you need the exact primitive.", content: guidance, code: "import (\"file\" \"http\" \"json\" \"csv\" \"db\" \"probability\" \"str\").\nrows := db.all(type: \"Customer\")\ntext := json.toText(data: rows)\nparsed := csv.parse(data: \"student,class\\nJohn,10c\")\nlabel := str.concat(left: \"rows json=\", right: text)\nmean := probability.mean(data: [2, 4, 6, 8])", note: "Core declaration files describe the Felidae surface; native code does the heavy work. Keep new heavy capabilities behind modules or helper methods instead of growing unrelated interpreter logic.", code2: "system.print(value: \"hello\")\nfile.readFile(path: \"data.txt\")\nfile.writeFile(path: \"out.fx\", data: \"Person(name: \\\"Alice\\\").\", mode: \"write\")\n\npage := HttpHtmlDocument(title: \"App\", body: \"<h1>Hello</h1>\")\nresponse := HttpHtmlResponse(body: page)\nHttpServeResponse(host: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 8090, response: response)")).
